/** @page overloading Overloading

    @tableofcontents

    @section overloading_intro Overloading Introduction
    Functions and methods can be overloaded if parameter types are declared as in the following example:

    @code{.py}
int sub example(int i) {
    printf("i=%d\n", i);
    return i + 1;
}

string sub example(string str) {
    printf("str=%s\n", str);
    return str + "foo";
}
    @endcode

    In this case, the first version (<tt>example(int)</tt>) will be executed if called with an integer argument, and the second (<tt>example(string)</tt>) if called with a string argument.

    %Qore looks at the arguments passed to the function or method and tries to find the best match.  A perfect match is when all arguments exactly match the expected types.  An imperfect match is when the declared type accepts the argument value, but it is not an exact match.  Examples of imperfect matches are @ref float_type "float" accepting an @ref int_type "int" value, or a @ref number_type "number" type accepting a @ref float_type "float".

    When types are declared in the source, %Qore uses this information to perform overloaded function and method resolution at parse time, which speeds up runtime execution.  If there is not enough information to match the function or method variant at parse time, then %Qore has to perform variant resolution at runtime, therefore it's advisable to declare types whenever possible.

    A variant is selected at parse time only when the declared types guarantee that it matches at run time; when an
    argument might not match it, as an argument returned as a reference from a call (a value of a
    <tt>reference&lt;</tt><em>type</em><tt>&gt;</tt> type, which a parameter of the referenced type does not accept),
    the variant is resolved at run time with the evaluated arguments.  When another variant accepts no value for an
    argument that the best match does not accept no value for, the best match is selected at parse time only if the
    argument always has a value: a literal value, a constant, a call, a new object, a closure or reference, a
    conditional expression of such expressions, or a local variable or parameter that is assigned on every path to
    the call and not removed or deleted since; for a member, a global variable, a variable of a closure or another
    expression, the variant is resolved at run time.  A variant selected so is resolved again at run time if the
    argument has no value after all, as for a method call that calls an overriding method whose return type accepts
    no value.  A variant selected at parse time can still not
    match at run time only because of the state of a value, which declared types do not describe: an argument that has
    no value (a member or variable of the type that was not assigned) or a deleted object; then the variant is resolved
    again at run time, as for a call resolved at run time, with the arguments as given.

    @section overloading_no_value Arguments With No Value And Overloading
    An argument with no value is an argument: it counts toward the number of arguments of the call, whether it is a
    literal @ref nothing "NOTHING" or a variable or expression holding no value, so the call selects the same variant
    at parse time and at run time.  For example, with <tt>f(int)</tt> and <tt>f(int, *string)</tt>, the calls
    <tt>f(1, NOTHING)</tt> and <tt>f(1, v)</tt>, with \c v holding no value, call <tt>f(int, *string)</tt>, while
    <tt>f(1)</tt> calls <tt>f(int)</tt>.  When several variants accept no value equally well, the first one declared
    is called; for example, with <tt>f(*string)</tt> declared before <tt>f(*softlist&lt;string&gt;)</tt>,
    <tt>f(NOTHING)</tt> and <tt>f(v)</tt> call <tt>f(*string)</tt>.

    The one exception is a call with a lone literal @ref nothing "NOTHING" argument, which is parsed as a call without
    arguments: <tt>v(NOTHING)</tt> is the same call as <tt>v()</tt>, while <tt>v(x)</tt>, with \c x holding no value,
    passes one argument.  This matters only when a variant takes variable arguments (<tt>...</tt>) or no parameters:
    with <tt>v(*string)</tt> and <tt>v(...)</tt>, <tt>v(NOTHING)</tt> and <tt>v()</tt> call <tt>v(...)</tt> with no
    arguments, and <tt>v(x)</tt> calls <tt>v(*string)</tt>.

    @section overloading_named_arguments Named Arguments And Overloading
    Named-argument calls are resolved by applying the argument names to each accessible candidate variant's own
    parameter list before the normal type scoring runs. A candidate is rejected if a name is unknown for that variant,
    appears more than once, or would overwrite a parameter already filled by a positional argument.

    @code{.py}
string sub describe(int count, string mode = "default") {
    return sprintf("count:%d:%s", count, mode);
}

string sub describe(float size, string unit = "m") {
    return sprintf("size:%s:%s", size, unit);
}

describe(count: 3);           # selects the first variant
describe(size: 1.5, unit: "cm");  # selects the second variant
    @endcode

    If more than one variant accepts the same named call with the same type score, variants with fewer omitted
    defaultable parameters are preferred. If the call is still ambiguous, the normal overload ambiguity diagnostics
    apply.

    Argument names are evaluated against each variant independently. A name that is valid for one overload does not
    make other overloads eligible:

    @code{.py}
string sub open(string path, string encoding = "UTF-8") {
    return "file";
}

string sub open(string host, int port) {
    return "socket";
}

open(path: "/tmp/data.txt");       # selects open(path, encoding)
open(host: "localhost", port: 80); # selects open(host, port)
    @endcode

    A positional argument still binds by position before named arguments are applied, so a later named argument cannot
    target the same parameter:

    @code{.py}
open("/tmp/data.txt", encoding: "ISO-8859-1"); # valid
open("/tmp/data.txt", path: "/tmp/other.txt"); # parse-time error
    @endcode

    @see @ref require-types "%require-types"

    @section overloading_untyped_containers Untyped Containers And Typed Container Parameters
    A list or hash built at runtime without a declared element type, such as a \c list<auto> or \c hash<auto>
    value, or a container made by a language binding, is accepted at runtime for a typed container parameter when
    every element is accepted by the parameter's element type; elements that are themselves untyped containers are
    checked the same way against a nested container type.  The argument is then converted to the parameter type as an
    assignment converts it; the caller's value is not changed.

    @code{.py}
string sub total(list<int> l) {
    return sprintf("%s: %d", l.fullType(), foldl $1 + $2, l);
}

string sub total(list<string> l) {
    return sprintf("%s: %s", l.fullType(), l.join(""));
}

auto ints = ();
push ints, 1;
push ints, 2;
auto strs = ();
push strs, "a";
total(ints);    # calls total(list<int>), which gets a list<int>
total(strs);    # calls total(list<string>), which gets a list<string>
    @endcode

    Such a match is weaker than one that needs no conversion: a variant whose parameter takes the value unchanged,
    such as \c list, \c list<auto> or \c softlist<auto>, is preferred, while a typed container variant is preferred
    to one with an \c auto or \c any parameter.  When several typed container variants accept the value with the
    same score, the one whose element type matches the elements best is called (ex: \c list<int> rather than
    \c list<number> for a list of integers), whatever their order of declaration.  A container with an element that
    the element type does not accept selects another variant, or raises \c RUNTIME-OVERLOAD-ERROR if no other
    variant matches.

    The elements of a typed container are checked too where its type alone does not decide whether it can be
    converted: a nested container that is accepted by its shape (ex: a \c list<list<string>> for a
    \c list<list<softint>> parameter, which converts the strings), a nested element type that may not match (ex: a
    \c list<list<auto>> for a \c list<list<int>> parameter), or a \c softlist<T> parameter, which converts any list.
    A variant whose binding would fail for the elements given is not selected; otherwise such a container is ranked
    by its type as before.  A typed container whose element type is the parameter's element type is not checked.

    An empty untyped container is accepted by every typed container parameter, and is not checked, so the first such
    variant declared is called.  A hash argument is not converted to a @ref hashdecl "hashdecl" type, as an
    assignment does not convert it, but a hash element of an untyped container is converted to a hashdecl element
    type as in an assignment, which reports a key that the hashdecl does not declare.

    @section overloaded_methods Overloaded Class Methods

    Class methods may also be overloaded, but note that \c destructor(), \c copy(), @ref methodGate_methods "methodGate()", @ref memberGate_methods "memberGate()", and @ref memberNotification_methods "memberNotification()" methods may not be overloaded, but all other methods may be overloaded.

    When resolving an overloaded method in a class hierarchy, the first method that matches the arguments is selected, so even if a better match might be declared in a parent class, if any method variant matches in a class, then that class's method is used.  The same rules are used with parse-time resolution as at runtime.

    When a class hierarchy has static and normal methods with the same name, the method is found by its name before
    the arguments are matched:
    - a call or call reference with a class prefix (<tt>Class::name()</tt>) finds a static method with that name
      anywhere in the hierarchy, and a normal method only if the hierarchy has no static method with that name; in
      code without @ref new-style "%new-style" or @ref allow-bare-refs "%allow-bare-refs", it finds only static
      methods
    - a call without a class prefix (<tt>name()</tt>) finds a normal method with that name anywhere in the hierarchy,
      and a static method only if the hierarchy has no normal method with that name

    Only the variants of the method found are matched against the arguments: a call whose arguments match only a
    method of the other kind raises a parse exception.  Since the whole hierarchy is searched before the other kind
    of method is considered, <tt>Child::p()</tt> calls a static \c p() method of a parent class even when \c Child
    declares a normal \c p() method.

    @subsection override_return_types Return Types of Overriding and Overloading Methods

    A call of a method on an object typed with a parent class is resolved at parse time to a variant of the parent
    class method, and its result is trusted to have that variant's declared return type; at run time the object's
    class can provide the variant that is called.  Therefore a child class method variant that such a call can select
    must return a compatible type, or the class is refused with a \c PARSE-TYPE-ERROR:
    - a variant that overrides a parent class variant (with the same parameter types), a variant that accepts
      arguments that the parent class variant accepts (an overload with broader or additional optional parameters),
      or a variant that a @ref named_arguments_virtual_dispatch "named call" of the parent class variant can select
      (it accepts the names of the parameters of the parent class variant that such a call gives, after the
      arguments that the call gives by position, with parameter types that can accept the same values, such as
      <tt>m(string b, int a)</tt> for <tt>m(int a, string b)</tt>, or <tt>m(int x, int y = 0, string b)</tt> for a
      call <tt>m(1, b: "s")</tt>),
      must return the parent class variant's type or a narrower type that it accepts without a conversion
      (covariant return types: a subclass of a returned class, \c int for \c *int); a type that would be converted,
      such as \c float for \c int, is refused
    - a parent class variant that returns @ref nothing allows any return type, as it promises no value to a call
    - the variants of all methods that calls of the class select before the parent class method are checked: with
      @ref multiple_inheritance_order "multiple inheritance", also the variants of a parent class listed before the
      class that declares the parent class method
    - in a class that inherits an instantiation of a generic class, the type arguments are substituted in the return
      types of the generic class's methods (<tt>T get()</tt> of <tt>Box&lt;int&gt;</tt> returns \c int); a generic child class that passes its own type parameter to the parent class must return it where the parent class
      method returns the type parameter, as an instantiation can bind it to any type
    - variants without a declared return type, static methods, and \c private:internal parent class methods (which
      are not inherited) are not checked
    - an overload that a parent class call cannot select, as its parameter types do not intersect and it does not
      accept the names of the parent class variant's parameters, can return any type

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
class Base {
    int m(int i) { return i; }
}
class Child inherits Base {
    # OK: an overload that a call typed against Base cannot select
    string m(string s) { return s; }
    # PARSE-TYPE-ERROR: a call b.m(1) for an object b typed Base can select it
    string m(softint i) { return "x"; }
}
    @endcode

    @see @ref qore_classes for more information
*/
